<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tatars</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tatar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tatars is a name applied to various Turkic-speaking peoples of the Eurasian steppe, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> who raided and fought along the borders of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> both fought against Tatar raiders and drew on Tatar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> traditions in developing its light cavalry. Accounts of the Commonwealth describe these frontier warrior cultures as an important military resource.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tatars.jpg" alt="Tatars" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tatar_intellectuals_in_the_Russian_Empire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That summer he besieged Jeremi Wiśniowiecki at Zbarazh and, with İslâm III Giray's <b>Tatars</b>, ambushed King John II Casimir near Zboriv; the king won over the khan, and Khmelnytsky had to negotiate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With Lithuanian support and the backing of the leading Crimean <b>Tatar</b> clans, he established an independent khanate in the Crimean peninsula and the neighbouring steppe around 1441; the exact date is uncertain, and some historians prefer 1443 or 1449.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of the steppe population was Kipchak Turkic, and by the fourteenth century the Mongol elite had adopted the Kipchak language and merged into the Turkic population that the Russians called <b>Tatars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Vytautas granted privileges to the Jews in 1388 and settled <b>Tatars</b> and Karaites near Trakai, communities whose descendants still live in Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When Tver rose against the <b>Tatars</b> in 1327, Yury's brother Ivan I Kalita joined the Horde's punitive expedition and was rewarded with the grand princely title, which he held alone from 1331.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Emerging from a fragmented world of rival Mongol and Turkic tribes, among them the <b>Tatars</b>, Keraits, Merkits and Naimans, he unified the steppe through alliances, military skill and ruthless suppression of opponents, and was proclaimed Genghis Khan at a kurultai (assembly) in 1206.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
